>C</given-names></name><name><surname>De Cesaris</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Scoglio</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Bouche</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Molinaro</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Aquino</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Zani</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>PKCalpha-mediated ERK, JNK and p38 activation regulates the myogenic program in human rhabdomyosarcoma cells</article-title><source>J Cell Sci</source><year>2002</year><volume>115</volume><fpage>3587</fpage><lpage>3599</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12186945</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1242/jcs.00037</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B41"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bradford</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A rapid and sensitive method for the quantitation of microgram quantities of protein utilizing the principle of protein-dye binding</article-title><source>Anal Biochem</source><year>1976</year><volume>72</volume><fpage>248</fpage><lpage>254</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">942051</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0003-2697(76)90527-3</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B42"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bouwman</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Renes</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Mariman</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A combination of protein profiling and isotopomer analysis using matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization-time of flight mass spectrometry reveals an active metabolism of the extracellular matrix of 3T3-L1 adipocytes</article-title><source>Proteomics</source><year>2004</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>3855</fpage><lpage>3863</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15540163</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/pmic.200400861</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B43"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bouwman</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Suylen</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Renes</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Mariman</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Evaluation and improving the success rate of protein identification by peptide mass fingerprinting using matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization time-of-flight mass spectrometry</article-title><source>Rapid Commun Mass Spectrom</source><year>2005</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>2465</fpage><lpage>2468</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16059887</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/rcm.2073</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B44"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Matrix-Science Mascot Search Engine</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.matrixscience.com"/></citation></ref><ref id="B45"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Swiss-Prot Database</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://expasy.ch/sprot"/></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p><bold>Unsupervised hierarchical clustering of protein profiles</bold>. A dendogram, generated by unsupervised hierarchical clustering of protein expression data of 2-D profiles of protein isolates obtained from three biological replicates of small intestinal scrapings, partially and fully differentiated Caco-2 cells, HT-29 cells, Hep G2 cells and TE 671 cells. Profiles of large intestine (1) and (2) come from one human sample.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-91-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><bold>PCA of protein profiles</bold>. PCA projection of protein profiles obtained by 2-DE in a scatter plot using the entire data set (A) and the data set after subtracting data points corresponding to proteins common for <italic>in vitro </italic>cell cultures (B). In panel A, PC1 and PC2 explain 37.3% and 13.8% of the total variation, respectively. In panel B, PC1 and PC2 explain 24.9% and 17.4% of the total variation, respectively. Open symbols and closed symbols represent 2-D profiles of cell lines and epithelial scrapings, respectively, as shown in panel A. The cluster numbers containing proteins which are mainly responsible for separation between groups are depicted next to the arrows in panel A. SI, small intestine; LI, large intestine; EC, epithelial cells.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-91-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><bold>Clustering of identified protein spots according to the expression levels in analyzed samples</bold>. Identified protein spots grouped in different clusters (cluster 1&#x02013;5) according to the expression level in different samples. Triplicate spot intensities are shown by a colour range; bright red, black, and bright green represent high, average, and low levels of protein expression.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-91-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p><bold>Clustering of identified protein spots according to the expression levels in analyzed samples (continuation of Figure 3)</bold>. Identified protein spots grouped in different clusters (cluster 5&#x02013;12) according to the expression level in different samples. Triplicate spot intensities are shown by a colour range; bright red, black, and bright green represent high, average, and low levels of protein expression.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-91-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p><bold>Protein profiles of differentiated Caco-2 cells and human small intestinal epithelial cells</bold>. 2-D pattern of 5-day-differentiated Caco-2 cells (A) and small intestinal epithelial scrapings (B). Identified protein spots are indicated with spot numbers corresponding to those in Figure 3 and 4. When a spot number is indicated in bold/italic, this means that the spot could not be observed in the particular pattern.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-91-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><bold>Protein profiles of human large intestinal epithelial cells, HT-29, Hep G2, and TE 671 cells</bold>. 2-D pattern of large intestinal epithelial scrapings (A), HT-29 cells (B), Hep G2 cells (C) and TE 671 cells (D).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-91-6"/></fig></sec></back></article>